 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.697,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "How does this cell leave your blood? This wall has no open door."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.5,
   "dur": 9.286,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "It's a neutrophil, the most common white cell in adults, about as wide as a red one. Under a sore fingertip, they pile up outside the wall."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 15.5,
   "dur": 10.057,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Rudolf Virchow taught that pus grew from the inflamed tissue. In eighteen sixty-seven, his assistant Julius Cohnheim watched a living frog's vessels. White cells climbed out."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 26.3,
   "dur": 14.805,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "Where you're hurt or infected, the wall turns sticky, and the cell rolls. Then its grips switch on, and it stops. In mice, it crawls to a seam in the wall. In a rare inherited condition, those grips are faulty. Cells roll on past."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 43.5,
   "dur": 9.852,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "This one wedges its nucleus into the seam, making its own door. It pours through a gap narrower than itself. In lab studies, the seam seals shut behind it."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 56.7,
   "dur": 6.009,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "So pus is mostly white cells from your blood. In mice, white cells open their own way out."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 63.5,
   "dur": 4.359,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Meanwhile, as you watched, your bones made more than ten million new ones."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 68.7,
   "dur": 3.779,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: season two. What sets off nearly every heartbeat?"
  }
 ]
}